# Root-cause location
File: jxl-encoder/src/effort.rs:1429-1436 (`EffortProfile::adapt_to_image_lossy`)
Guard:
    if pixels < LOSSY_SMALL_IMAGE_PIXEL_THRESHOLD  // 500_000
        && distance < LOSSY_LOW_DISTANCE_THRESHOLD  // 2.0
        && self.try_dct64
    {
        self.try_dct64 = false;
    }
Result: 1418519 (512×512 = 262_144 px < 500_000) at d ∈ {1.0, 1.2, 1.6} (< 2.0)
        → try_dct64 = false → find_best_64x64_transform never runs → 0 % DCT64 pick rate.
        cjxl picks DCT64 on 48-53 % of 8×8-block-area on the same image.

# Calibration provenance gap
adapt_to_image_lossy was tuned by W44-17 chunk (commit 142ef4f6 family) on a SINGLE image,
7256805.png (CID22 training), at the same small_0.26MP (512×512) cell. Bench data:
  benchmarks/vardct_ac_dispatch_paired_2026-05-17.tsv

7256805 at d=1.0 e=7: variant A (DCT64 off) = 27199, variant B (DCT64 on) = 27637.
  → A 1.6% SMALLER on 7256805 → "DCT64 isn't winning here" conclusion.
1418519 at d=1.0 e=7: forcing variant-B (DCT64 on) = 21723 vs default 23074.
  → B 5.86% SMALLER on 1418519 → conclusion was image-specific, not size-specific.

The wedge: 1418519 is a smooth-photo (pool water + sky + distant mountains, kurtosis 3.18)
whereas 7256805 is more textured. cjxl's per-block cost model picks DCT64 wherever the
content is sufficiently smooth — and small smooth-photo cells exist that the W44-17
calibration set didn't sample.

